<article class="env-theorem" id="0D5A">
        
      
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0D5A">Theorem <span data-tag="0D5A">99.15.11</span> <span class="named">(Algebraicity of the stack of curves)</span>.</a> The stack $\mathcal{C}\! \mathit{urves}$ (Situation <a href="/tag/0D4Z" data-tag="0D4Z">99.15.1</a>) is algebraic. In fact, for any algebraic space $B$ the stack $B\text{-}\mathcal{C}\! \mathit{urves}$ (Remark <a href="/tag/0D54" data-tag="0D54">99.15.5</a>) is algebraic. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The absolute case follows from Artin's Axioms, Lemma <a href="/tag/07Y4" data-tag="07Y4">98.17.1</a> and Lemmas <a href="/tag/0D53" data-tag="0D53">99.15.4</a>, <a href="/tag/0D56" data-tag="0D56">99.15.7</a>, <a href="/tag/0D55" data-tag="0D55">99.15.6</a>, <a href="/tag/0D58" data-tag="0D58">99.15.9</a>, and <a href="/tag/0D59" data-tag="0D59">99.15.10</a>. The case over $B$ follows from this, the description of $B\text{-}\mathcal{C}\! \mathit{urves}$ as a $2$-fibre product in Remark <a href="/tag/0D54" data-tag="0D54">99.15.5</a>, and the fact that algebraic stacks have $2$-fibre products, see Algebraic Stacks, Lemma <a href="/tag/04T2" data-tag="04T2">94.14.3</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>